1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,420
In J.R.R.'s world, Gandalf is one of five wizards sent by the Valar to guide the inhabitants of Middle-earth in their struggles against the dark forces Sauron.

2
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:28,860
Gandalf's body was mortal, subject to the physical rules of Middle-earth, but his spirit was immortal, as seen when he died as Gandalf the Grey and resurrected as Gandalf the White.

3
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:41,040
According to the Wachowski script, an awakened human only has to link up and hack the neon binary code of the Matrix to learn how to fly a helicopter in a matter of seconds.

4
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:48,040
Or if you are the one, or one of the ones, you don't even need a helicopter. You just need a cool pair of shades.

5
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:58,040
Cheshire cats can juggle their own heads. iPads are rudimentary. No Quidditch match ends until the Golden Snitch is caught.

6
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:04,280
And the answer to the ultimate question of life, the universe, and everything is most certainly 42.

7
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:11,240
Just like real life, fictional worlds operate consistently within a spectrum of physical and societal rules.

8
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:16,260
That's what makes these intricate worlds believable, comprehensible, and worth exploring.

9
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:24,180
In real life, the Law of Gravity holds seven book sets of Harry Potter to millions of bookshelves around the world.

10
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:25,780
We know this to be true.

11
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:28,840
But we also know that ever since J.K.

12
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:39,440
typed the words wizard, wand, and Wingardium Leviosa, that Law of Gravity has ceased to exist on the trillions of pages resting between those bookends.

13
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,360
Authors of science fiction and fantasy literally build worlds.

14
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:56,780
They make rules, maps, lineages, languages, cultures, universes, alternate universes within universes, and from those worlds sprout story after story after story.

15
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:58,840
When it's done well, readers can read the book.

16
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:03,860
And even the most experienced can understand fictional worlds and their rules just as well as the characters that live in them do,

17
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:10,160
and sometimes just as well or even better than the reader understands the world outside of the book.

18
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:11,840
But how?

19
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:19,440
How can human-made squiggles on a page reflect lights into our eyes that send signals to our brains

20
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:27,440
that we logically and emotionally decode as complex narratives that move us to fight, cry, sing, and think,

21
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:28,740
that are strong enough to make us feel like we're living in a world of chaos, and that we have to fight for ourselves and our lives?

22
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:29,740
How can we make the world of fiction and fantasy a world of fantasy,

23
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:32,740
that is strong enough not only to hold up a world that is completely invented by the author,

24
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:40,740
but also to change the reader's perspective on the real world that resumes only when the final squiggle is reached?

25
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:48,740
I'm not sure anyone knows the answer to that question, yet fantastical fictional worlds are created every day,

26
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:53,740
in our minds, on computers, even on napkins at the restaurant down the street.

27
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:56,740
The truth is, your imagination and a willingness to figuratively live in your imagination,

28
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:57,740
are the ones that make the world of fiction and fantasy so special.

29
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:58,740
The world of fiction and fantasy is the world of fantasy, and the world of fiction is the world of fantasy.

30
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:08,640
Every human being, every current human being knows there are plenty of places within the world for them to create and make意

31
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:10,940
To create it, you need to know where you live,

32
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:12,040
and where they're going to,

33
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,540
challenge, and cited,

34
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:14,440
and also,

35
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:14,760
hit,

36
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:16,100
bite what's best,

37
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:19,440
and use what they see to,

38
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:21,740
to grow up,

39
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:23,040
to be a Kir pulp,

40
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:25,040
to be a queen,

41
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,740
and always to rule.

42
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:34,740
I like to create a timeline showing how the world came to be, what past events have shaped the way it is now.

43
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:40,240
Then I brainstorm answers to questions that draw out the details of my fictional world.

44
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:42,320
What rules are in place here?

45
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:50,200
This covers everything from laws of gravity, or not, to the rules of society and the punishments for individuals who break them.

46
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,360
What kind of government does this world have?

47
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:55,440
Who has power and who doesn't?

48
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:57,740
What do people believe in here?

49
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,600
And what does this society value most?

50
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:03,340
Then it's time to think about day-to-day life.

51
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:05,440
What's the weather like in this world?

52
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,740
Where do the inhabitants live and work and go to school?

53
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:11,020
What do they eat and how do they play?

54
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:13,520
How do they treat their young and their old?

55
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:17,480
What relationships do they have with the animals and plants of the world?

56
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:20,240
And what do those animals and plants look like?

57
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:22,740
What kind of technology exists?

58
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:23,660
Transportation?

59
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:24,800
Communication?

60
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:26,820
Access to information?

61
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:28,880
There's so much to think about.

62
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:33,860
So, spend some time living in those tasks and the answers to those questions,

63
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:37,320
and you're well on your way to building your own fictional world.

64
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:40,780
Once you know your world as well as you hope your reader will,

65
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:44,300
set your characters free in it and see what happens.

66
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:50,260
And ask yourself, how does this world you created shape the individuals who live in it,

67
00:04:50,620 --> 00:04:53,380
and what kind of conflict is likely to emerge?

68
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:55,020
Answer those questions,

69
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:56,700
and you have your story.

70
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:58,740
Good luck, future world builder!

71
00:04:58,740 --> 00:04:59,900
